::ENG1_U03_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: First, the robotics team sketches a design. Next, members build a model. Finally, they test the model and revise weak parts. Question: Which text structure is used? {
=A. Sequence#Correct. The words "First," "Next," and "Finally" organize the information as steps in order.
~B. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects or explain similarities and differences.
~C. Problem and solution#Incorrect. The passage does not focus on a problem followed by a solution.
~D. Cause and effect#Incorrect. The passage lists ordered steps instead of explaining why one event causes another.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Too much screen light before bed can delay sleep. One solution is to stop using bright devices thirty minutes before bedtime. Another solution is to use settings that reduce blue light. Question: Which structure is used? {
~A. Description#Incorrect. The passage does more than describe screen light; it presents a problem and responses.
=B. Problem and solution#Correct. The passage identifies a sleep problem and then gives possible solutions.
~C. Chronological biography#Incorrect. The passage is not about a person's life in time order.
~D. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not mainly compare two different subjects.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A rain garden collects water after storms. As water soaks into the soil, fewer puddles form on nearby sidewalks. Question: Which structure best fits the passage? {
~A. Definition only#Incorrect. The passage explains more than a definition because it shows what happens after water collects.
~B. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects.
=C. Cause and effect#Correct. The passage explains that water soaking into soil causes fewer puddles nearby.
~D. Question and answer#Incorrect. The passage does not ask and answer a question.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Heading: "Two Ways to Travel." The paragraph explains that buses can carry many passengers at once, while bicycles allow riders to choose flexible routes. Question: Which structure is signaled? {
~A. Problem and solution#Incorrect. The passage does not present a problem and proposed fix.
~B. Sequence#Incorrect. The passage does not list steps in order.
~C. Cause and effect#Incorrect. The passage does not explain how one event causes another.
=D. Compare and contrast#Correct. The passage presents two travel methods and explains how they differ.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Caption: A student labels seedlings before recording their height in a notebook. The article explains how classroom gardens support hands-on science. Question: What is the purpose of the caption? {
=A. It explains how the image connects to hands-on science.#Correct. The caption identifies the activity in the photo and links it to the article's topic.
~B. It proves all students enjoy gardening.#Incorrect. The caption shows one activity, not what all students feel.
~C. It gives the entire central idea of the article.#Incorrect. A caption supports the article but does not replace the full text.
~D. It changes the text into fiction.#Incorrect. A caption is an informational text feature and does not make the article fictional.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Bold term: compost. Sentence: Compost is decayed plant material that can enrich soil. Question: How does the bold term help the reader? {
~A. It hides the most important word.#Incorrect. Bold print usually highlights a term rather than hiding it.
~B. It shows the sentence is dialogue.#Incorrect. Bold print does not indicate spoken dialogue here.
=C. It signals a vocabulary term that the sentence defines.#Correct. The bold word draws attention to a key term and the sentence explains its meaning.
~D. It proves the passage is organized as a poem.#Incorrect. Bold vocabulary does not create poetic structure.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A chart lists three clubs, meeting days, and room numbers. The article explains how students can find activities after school. Question: Why is the chart useful? {
~A. It makes the article harder to scan.#Incorrect. A chart usually makes grouped information easier to scan.
=B. It organizes schedule details so readers can compare options quickly.#Correct. The chart helps readers locate days and rooms without searching through paragraphs.
~C. It replaces all written explanations.#Incorrect. A chart supports the article but does not replace all context.
~D. It proves every student must join all clubs.#Incorrect. The chart gives options, not a requirement.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A section titled "Before the Trip" explains packing water and maps. A section titled "During the Trip" explains staying with the group. Question: How do the headings support the structure? {
~A. They show cause and effect between water and maps.#Incorrect. The headings are based on time periods, not causes.
~B. They make the passage a personal narrative.#Incorrect. Headings can appear in informational text and do not make it narrative.
~C. They remove the need to read details.#Incorrect. Headings guide reading, but details still matter.
=D. They divide the information into time-based stages.#Correct. "Before" and "During" organize the information by when actions happen.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "Unlike paper maps, digital maps can update traffic conditions in real time. However, paper maps do not need batteries or a signal." Question: Which structure is used? {
=A. Compare and contrast#Correct. The passage explains differences between paper maps and digital maps.
~B. Problem and solution#Incorrect. The passage does not present one problem and a specific solution.
~C. Sequence#Incorrect. The passage does not list steps in order.
~D. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not simply define a term.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "When sidewalks are cracked, people using wheelchairs or strollers may have trouble moving safely. Repairing the sidewalks can make the route smoother for more people." Question: Which structure is used? {
~A. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects.
~B. Sequence#Incorrect. The passage does not present steps in time order.
~C. Description only#Incorrect. The passage moves from a problem to a repair.
=D. Problem and solution#Correct. The passage identifies unsafe cracked sidewalks and explains repair as a solution.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Sidebar: "Try This: Turn off lights when leaving a room." The main article explains ways families can save energy. Question: What does the sidebar do? {
~A. It introduces a fictional character.#Incorrect. The sidebar gives advice and does not create a story character.
=B. It gives a practical action related to the article topic.#Correct. The sidebar offers a quick tip connected to saving energy.
~C. It contradicts the topic of energy use.#Incorrect. Turning off lights supports the energy-saving topic.
~D. It lists unrelated sports scores.#Incorrect. The sidebar is about energy, not sports.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "Because native plants are adapted to local rainfall, they often need less watering than imported plants." Question: Which structure is most evident? {
~A. Sequence#Incorrect. The sentence does not present steps in order.
~B. Compare and biography#Incorrect. Biography is not used, and the focus is cause and effect.
=C. Cause and effect#Correct. The sentence explains why native plants often need less watering.
~D. Problem and solution#Incorrect. No problem and solution are developed.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Heading: "What Is a Watershed?" The paragraph defines a watershed as an area of land where water drains into the same river, lake, or ocean. Question: What does the heading prepare readers for? {
=A. A definition or explanation of the term#Correct. The heading uses "What Is" to signal that the section will explain a concept.
~B. A list of fictional characters#Incorrect. The heading signals an explanation of a term, not characters.
~C. A debate with two opposing claims#Incorrect. The heading asks what something is, not whether a claim is right.
~D. A recipe with ingredients#Incorrect. The passage explains a geographic term, not a recipe.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "Some students study best in quiet rooms. Others focus better with soft background noise. Both groups benefit from choosing a study space that fits their needs." Question: Which structure is used? {
~A. Problem and solution#Incorrect. The passage does not identify a problem and propose a fix.
=B. Compare and contrast#Correct. The passage compares two study preferences and shows a shared idea.
~C. Chronological order#Incorrect. The passage does not describe events in time order.
~D. Cause and effect#Incorrect. The passage does not mainly explain why one event causes another.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A timeline shows when a town built its first library, expanded the building, added computers, and opened a teen study room. Question: What does the timeline help readers understand? {
~A. The exact feelings of every library visitor#Incorrect. A timeline shows events, not every visitor's feelings.
~B. The meaning of every vocabulary word#Incorrect. A timeline organizes dates and events, not definitions.
=C. The order of changes in the library over time#Correct. A timeline is designed to show when events happened.
~D. The author's favorite book#Incorrect. The timeline gives historical information, not a personal preference.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "A drought can lower water levels in lakes. Lower water levels can reduce boat access and affect fish habitats." Question: Which structure is used? {
~A. Description only#Incorrect. The passage explains effects of drought rather than only describing it.
~B. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects.
~C. Question and answer#Incorrect. The passage does not pose a question.
=D. Cause and effect#Correct. The passage explains effects caused by drought and lower water levels.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: An article about healthy lunches includes a table with columns labeled "Food," "Nutrient," and "Why It Helps." Question: How does the table help readers? {
=A. It organizes related details so readers can connect foods to benefits.#Correct. The table lets readers match each food with a nutrient and its purpose.
~B. It turns the article into a fictional story.#Incorrect. A table is an informational feature, not a fiction marker.
~C. It hides the most important information in paragraphs.#Incorrect. A table makes information easier to see, not hidden.
~D. It proves every lunch must include the same food.#Incorrect. The table gives information, not a required menu.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "To make a simple budget, first list income. Next, write down regular expenses. Then compare the two amounts and decide how much can be saved." Question: Which clue most clearly signals structure? {
~A. The word "budget"#Incorrect. The topic is budgeting, but the structure clue is the ordering language.
~B. The phrase "simple budget"#Incorrect. This describes the topic but does not show organization.
=C. The words "first," "next," and "then"#Correct. These transition words clearly signal sequence.
~D. The word "income"#Incorrect. This is a content word, not the main structure signal.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A paragraph explains that school announcements were hard to hear in the cafeteria. Then it explains that the school added visual announcement screens near the lunch lines. Question: Which structure is used? {
~A. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects.
=B. Problem and solution#Correct. The passage identifies an announcement problem and then describes a response.
~C. Chronological biography#Incorrect. The passage is not about a person's life.
~D. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define a term.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: Glossary entry: "evaporation - the process by which liquid water changes into water vapor." Question: What is the purpose of the glossary entry? {
~A. To tell a personal story about water#Incorrect. A glossary entry defines a term rather than telling a personal story.
~B. To compare two authors' opinions#Incorrect. The entry gives a definition, not a comparison of opinions.
~C. To provide a plot twist#Incorrect. Plot twists belong to narratives, not glossary entries.
=D. To define a key term for readers#Correct. The entry explains the meaning of "evaporation."
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "Indoor plants may improve a room's appearance. They can also remind people to care for living things. However, plants need regular watering and enough light." Question: Which structure is used? {
=A. Description with benefits and needs#Correct. The passage describes indoor plants by explaining positive qualities and care needs.
~B. Sequence#Incorrect. The passage does not present steps in order.
~C. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define a single term.
~D. Biography#Incorrect. The passage is not about a person's life.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A map in an article marks bike paths, bus stops, and crosswalks near a school. Question: Why would this map be useful? {
~A. It explains the author's childhood.#Incorrect. A map shows places and routes, not a personal history.
=B. It helps readers see locations and routes mentioned in the article.#Correct. The map visually organizes where paths, stops, and crosswalks are located.
~C. It replaces the need for safe transportation.#Incorrect. A map informs readers but does not physically replace transportation.
~D. It proves all routes are the same distance.#Incorrect. The passage does not say the map proves equal distances.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "Unlike email, a handwritten note may feel more personal. Email, however, can arrive almost instantly." Question: Which words signal contrast? {
~A. "handwritten note" and "email"#Incorrect. These are the subjects being compared, but they are not the contrast signal words.
~B. "arrive" and "instantly"#Incorrect. These describe one feature but do not signal the contrast by themselves.
=C. "Unlike" and "however"#Correct. These words signal differences between handwritten notes and email.
~D. "personal" and "almost"#Incorrect. These words add detail, but they are not the clearest structure signals.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: A sidebar titled "Safety Reminder" says, "Wear gloves when handling sharp objects during a cleanup." Question: What is the sidebar's purpose? {
~A. It explains the entire history of cleanups.#Incorrect. The sidebar gives one reminder, not a full history.
~B. It changes the article into a play.#Incorrect. A sidebar is an informational feature, not a dramatic script.
~C. It removes all risks from the activity.#Incorrect. A reminder helps readers act safely but does not remove every risk.
=D. It gives a reminder connected to safe cleanup behavior.#Correct. The sidebar provides a focused safety tip related to the cleanup topic.
}

::ENG1_U03_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.05] Passage: "When a community center extended its evening hours, more students could use the study room after sports practice." Question: Which structure is most evident? {
~A. Compare and contrast#Incorrect. The passage does not compare two subjects.
=B. Cause and effect#Correct. Extending evening hours causes more students to be able to use the study room.
~C. Sequence#Incorrect. The passage does not list multiple steps in order.
~D. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define a term.
}
